WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:03.778
Flick asks a practical question: can one

2
00:00:03.778 --> 00:00:06.193
typed language describe a browser

3
00:00:06.193 --> 00:00:08.851
interface, a server, and a command-line

4
00:00:08.851 --> 00:00:09.414
program?

5
00:00:09.734 --> 00:00:11.746
This repository brings those paths

6
00:00:11.746 --> 00:00:14.026
together through a shared compiler and

7
00:00:14.026 --> 00:00:15.702
explicit runtime boundaries.

8
00:00:16.022 --> 00:00:18.268
The interesting part is not just the

9
00:00:18.268 --> 00:00:18.717
syntax.

10
00:00:19.037 --> 00:00:21.608
It is how application code, effects,

11
00:00:21.608 --> 00:00:24.446
libraries, and generated programs fit

12
00:00:24.446 --> 00:00:25.155
together.

13
00:00:25.475 --> 00:00:27.862
We will follow those boundaries, using the

14
00:00:27.862 --> 00:00:30.248
current source tree rather than assuming

15
00:00:30.248 --> 00:00:32.225
every target behaves identically.

16
00:00:33.495 --> 00:00:36.343
First, find the center of the repository.

17
00:00:36.663 --> 00:00:38.983
The native Flick command and the browser's

18
00:00:38.983 --> 00:00:41.303
WebAssembly interface both call the Rust

19
00:00:41.303 --> 00:00:42.098
compiler core.

20
00:00:42.418 --> 00:00:45.087
Around that center sit smaller crates for

21
00:00:45.087 --> 00:00:47.603
shared intermediate representations,

22
00:00:47.603 --> 00:00:49.128
formatting, and plugins.

23
00:00:49.448 --> 00:00:52.150
Execution lives elsewhere, in runtime

24
00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:52.826
packages.

25
00:00:53.146 --> 00:00:55.634
This separation matters when navigating a

26
00:00:55.634 --> 00:00:56.037
change.

27
00:00:56.357 --> 00:00:59.094
A browser feature can share checking logic

28
00:00:59.094 --> 00:01:01.682
with the command line without sharing its

29
00:01:01.682 --> 00:01:04.049
filesystem access, package loading, or

30
00:01:04.049 --> 00:01:05.528
execution environment.

31
00:01:06.798 --> 00:01:09.258
Next, look at a function signature.

32
00:01:09.578 --> 00:01:12.101
In the first-slice example, describe takes

33
00:01:12.101 --> 00:01:14.623
a string and declares asynchronous work

34
00:01:14.623 --> 00:01:16.498
plus two service requirements.

35
00:01:16.818 --> 00:01:19.137
Its body handles a typed not-found error

36
00:01:19.137 --> 00:01:21.388
instead of treating every failure as an

37
00:01:21.388 --> 00:01:22.820
unstructured exception.

38
00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:25.554
Services are supplied at the application

39
00:01:25.554 --> 00:01:26.106
boundary.

40
00:01:26.426 --> 00:01:28.175
Regions add another dimension by

41
00:01:28.175 --> 00:01:30.300
constraining how resources and running

42
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.799
work can escape their owners.

43
00:01:32.119 --> 00:01:34.543
These declarations connect the language's

44
00:01:34.543 --> 00:01:36.764
static checks to the lifetime rules its

45
00:01:36.764 --> 00:01:38.111
runtimes must preserve.

46
00:01:39.381 --> 00:01:41.427
Now follow a source file through the

47
00:01:41.427 --> 00:01:41.956
compiler.

48
00:01:42.276 --> 00:01:44.868
Parsing keeps a lossless syntax tree, so

49
00:01:44.868 --> 00:01:47.156
source details remain available to

50
00:01:47.156 --> 00:01:48.986
formatting and diagnostics.

51
00:01:49.306 --> 00:01:52.126
Resolution establishes names and exports.

52
00:01:52.446 --> 00:01:55.168
Checking determines types, effects, and

53
00:01:55.168 --> 00:01:57.807
regions, while recording metadata for

54
00:01:57.807 --> 00:01:58.715
later phases.

55
00:01:59.035 --> 00:02:01.202
Lowering then makes those decisions

56
00:02:01.202 --> 00:02:03.090
explicit in typed intermediate

57
00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:04.069
representation.

58
00:02:04.389 --> 00:02:06.287
Release builds may optimize that

59
00:02:06.287 --> 00:02:08.185
representation before emission.

60
00:02:08.505 --> 00:02:10.929
JavaScript, TypeScript, and Go backends

61
00:02:10.929 --> 00:02:13.207
consume checked meaning, rather than

62
00:02:13.207 --> 00:02:15.631
independently guessing it from source

63
00:02:15.631 --> 00:02:16.219
spelling.

64
00:02:17.489 --> 00:02:19.801
The intermediate representation is a

65
00:02:19.801 --> 00:02:21.553
useful contributor landmark.

66
00:02:21.873 --> 00:02:24.712
It distinguishes pure calls, synchronous

67
00:02:24.712 --> 00:02:27.469
calls, and runtime calls that may suspend.

68
00:02:27.789 --> 00:02:30.162
Those stages affect when work happens,

69
00:02:30.162 --> 00:02:32.017
even when two signatures look

70
00:02:32.017 --> 00:02:33.500
superficially similar.

71
00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.345
The checker also records dictionary

72
00:02:36.345 --> 00:02:38.789
arguments, resolved identities, and

73
00:02:38.789 --> 00:02:39.522
coercions.

74
00:02:39.842 --> 00:02:42.252
Lowering carries that information forward.

75
00:02:42.572 --> 00:02:44.985
Optimization must preserve evaluation

76
00:02:44.985 --> 00:02:46.830
order and observable failures.

77
00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:49.225
You can remember this boundary as

78
00:02:49.225 --> 00:02:51.596
decisions made once, then represented

79
00:02:51.596 --> 00:02:53.449
explicitly for every backend.

80
00:02:54.719 --> 00:02:57.350
A real project adds context around that

81
00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:57.969
pipeline.

82
00:02:58.289 --> 00:03:00.864
The Host interface supplies source access

83
00:03:00.864 --> 00:03:03.081
and plugin access without making the

84
00:03:03.081 --> 00:03:05.442
compiler core open native files itself.

85
00:03:05.762 --> 00:03:08.115
Native commands combine manifests,

86
00:03:08.115 --> 00:03:10.861
dependencies, and source roots through a

87
00:03:10.861 --> 00:03:11.959
filesystem host.

88
00:03:12.279 --> 00:03:14.568
Editor analysis can overlay unsaved

89
00:03:14.568 --> 00:03:16.783
buffers on those same project rules.

90
00:03:17.103 --> 00:03:19.426
One important distinction follows:

91
00:03:19.426 --> 00:03:22.291
single-file checking can trust unavailable

92
00:03:22.291 --> 00:03:24.924
imports, while project checking follows

93
00:03:24.924 --> 00:03:25.699
interfaces.

94
00:03:26.019 --> 00:03:27.937
Use the project entry point when

95
00:03:27.937 --> 00:03:30.565
investigating an actual application build.

96
00:03:31.835 --> 00:03:34.317
Runtime selection is a separate decision,

97
00:03:34.317 --> 00:03:36.523
and the current defaults deserve care.

98
00:03:36.843 --> 00:03:39.479
Native projects select Flick's own runtime

99
00:03:39.479 --> 00:03:40.138
by default.

100
00:03:40.458 --> 00:03:42.918
The browser compiler explicitly selects

101
00:03:42.918 --> 00:03:44.535
the Effect adapter instead.

102
00:03:44.855 --> 00:03:47.153
Go output uses its own whole-program

103
00:03:47.153 --> 00:03:48.456
emitter and runtime.

104
00:03:48.776 --> 00:03:51.309
The optional adapter also supports shaped

105
00:03:51.309 --> 00:03:53.280
foreign crossings, but it is not a

106
00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.672
promise that every external capability

107
00:03:55.672 --> 00:03:56.728
works everywhere.

108
00:03:57.048 --> 00:03:59.541
Follow the configured backend and the

109
00:03:59.541 --> 00:04:02.268
binding policy together, especially when

110
00:04:02.268 --> 00:04:04.606
debugging cancellation, cleanup, or

111
00:04:04.606 --> 00:04:05.619
foreign values.

112
00:04:06.889 --> 00:04:09.762
The language surface mostly lives in Flick

113
00:04:09.762 --> 00:04:12.475
libraries, not only in Rust compiler code.

114
00:04:12.795 --> 00:04:15.403
Core modules provide foundational data and

115
00:04:15.403 --> 00:04:16.743
language definitions.

116
00:04:17.063 --> 00:04:19.112
The standard library builds out

117
00:04:19.112 --> 00:04:21.540
application areas such as networking,

118
00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.665
reactive interfaces, and command

119
00:04:23.665 --> 00:04:24.576
descriptions.

120
00:04:24.896 --> 00:04:27.229
First-party plugins expand structured

121
00:04:27.229 --> 00:04:28.997
declarations for those areas.

122
00:04:29.317 --> 00:04:31.707
Their current heads are visible here, but

123
00:04:31.707 --> 00:04:33.956
the pattern is more important than the

124
00:04:33.956 --> 00:04:36.276
list: packages and plugins own reusable

125
00:04:36.276 --> 00:04:38.595
application behavior, while the shared

126
00:04:38.595 --> 00:04:40.704
compiler coordinates checking and

127
00:04:40.704 --> 00:04:42.391
generated-source locations.

128
00:04:43.661 --> 00:04:46.046
The browser tools show these boundaries in

129
00:04:46.046 --> 00:04:46.576
practice.

130
00:04:46.896 --> 00:04:49.362
Flick owns interactive state and event

131
00:04:49.362 --> 00:04:52.127
policies, while TypeScript handles browser

132
00:04:52.127 --> 00:04:54.144
and compiler interoperability.

133
00:04:54.464 --> 00:04:56.908
A persistent compiler worker retains a

134
00:04:56.908 --> 00:04:59.421
WebAssembly session as the source changes.

135
00:04:59.741 --> 00:05:02.306
Running the result is separate: ordinary

136
00:05:02.306 --> 00:05:04.720
programs use a disposable worker, and

137
00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.436
interface previews use a sandboxed frame.

138
00:05:07.756 --> 00:05:09.423
Shared code is checked without

139
00:05:09.423 --> 00:05:10.961
automatically executing it.

140
00:05:11.281 --> 00:05:13.616
This diagram describes source-level

141
00:05:13.616 --> 00:05:16.176
architecture, not a live browser test or

142
00:05:16.176 --> 00:05:17.909
a security certification.

143
00:05:19.179 --> 00:05:21.214
How does the project check its own

144
00:05:21.214 --> 00:05:21.650
claims?

145
00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:24.292
Independent fixtures state expected

146
00:05:24.292 --> 00:05:24.873
behavior.

147
00:05:25.193 --> 00:05:27.572
A separate Rust reference model provides

148
00:05:27.572 --> 00:05:29.290
another checker and evaluator.

149
00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:31.987
Lean developments establish results for

150
00:05:31.987 --> 00:05:34.025
stated mathematical contracts and

151
00:05:34.025 --> 00:05:34.772
assumptions.

152
00:05:35.092 --> 00:05:37.837
These are complementary forms of evidence,

153
00:05:37.837 --> 00:05:39.667
not interchangeable badges.

154
00:05:39.987 --> 00:05:41.893
A theorem about the model does not

155
00:05:41.893 --> 00:05:44.193
prove the production compiler or runtime

156
00:05:44.193 --> 00:05:44.653
correct.

157
00:05:44.973 --> 00:05:47.421
Semantic changes therefore require an

158
00:05:47.421 --> 00:05:49.942
explicit decision about model and proof

159
00:05:49.942 --> 00:05:52.093
impact, alongside implementation

160
00:05:52.093 --> 00:05:54.614
regressions and relevant backend tests.

161
00:05:55.884 --> 00:05:58.117
The measured checkout is substantial and

162
00:05:58.117 --> 00:05:59.648
deliberately multilingual.

163
00:05:59.968 --> 00:06:02.199
Rust implements the compiler, Flick

164
00:06:02.199 --> 00:06:04.653
implements libraries and interactive

165
00:06:04.653 --> 00:06:07.331
application code, and TypeScript connects

166
00:06:07.331 --> 00:06:09.265
execution and browser systems.

167
00:06:09.585 --> 00:06:12.165
Go supplies another runtime, while Lean

168
00:06:12.165 --> 00:06:13.651
states formal results.

169
00:06:13.971 --> 00:06:16.125
This snapshot contains roughly four

170
00:06:16.125 --> 00:06:18.488
hundred thousand physical source lines.

171
00:06:18.808 --> 00:06:21.154
The chart shows the largest language

172
00:06:21.154 --> 00:06:23.500
groups, including tests and fixtures.

173
00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:26.243
These are inventory measurements, not

174
00:06:26.243 --> 00:06:28.894
performance results, execution coverage,

175
00:06:28.894 --> 00:06:31.167
or a claim that the current working

176
00:06:31.167 --> 00:06:31.924
tree builds.

177
00:06:33.194 --> 00:06:35.919
Finally, choose a contributor entry point

178
00:06:35.919 --> 00:06:38.039
by the contract you want to change.

179
00:06:38.359 --> 00:06:40.686
Syntax work reaches formatting,

180
00:06:40.686 --> 00:06:43.100
diagnostics, and editor grammars.

181
00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:45.588
Runtime work reaches cancellation and

182
00:06:45.588 --> 00:06:46.704
resource ownership.

183
00:06:47.024 --> 00:06:49.576
Project work reaches package resolution

184
00:06:49.576 --> 00:06:51.180
and artifact publication.

185
00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:54.063
Reproduce behavior, add an independently

186
00:06:54.063 --> 00:06:56.553
expected regression, then run the checks

187
00:06:56.553 --> 00:06:57.652
for that boundary.

188
00:06:57.972 --> 00:07:00.519
The useful mental model is one shared

189
00:07:00.519 --> 00:07:03.395
compiler, explicit execution choices, and

190
00:07:03.395 --> 00:07:05.531
contracts carried across both.

191
00:07:05.851 --> 00:07:08.318
This overview reflects a dirty source

192
00:07:08.318 --> 00:07:11.083
snapshot; executable validation remains a

193
00:07:11.083 --> 00:07:11.980
separate task.

